{ Capablanca's endgame school. Small edges decide these: a spare pawn move, the right square for your king. Start by pushing your pawn to e3. [%cal Ye2e3] } 2. e3 { Your pawn move looks like nothing, but it hands him the move: he has to step aside now, and your king slides through to d5 next. [%cal Ge2e3] } (2. Kf4 { Your king can walk to f4 first instead of pushing the pawn. The order barely matters yet, so the result still holds. } 2... Kf6 { Also winning. Your king can take this route and still keep the pawn move in reserve for later. [%cal Be6f6] }) (2. Kd4 { The square d4 suits your king just as well as f4: either one keeps the win, since the pawn tempo is still unspent. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. This way works too, and the pawn move is still there when you need it. [%cal Be6d6] }) (2. Kf3 { Your king can drift to f3 instead, but with no pawn move left in hand he has nothing to answer his mirroring king with. } 2... Kf5 3. Ke3 3... Ke5 4. Kf3 4... Kf5 { The result has changed: shuffling the king alone lets him mirror you forever, and the win slips away without the pawn move in hand. [%cal Re5f5] }) (2. Ke3 { The e3 square looks natural, but it burns your one spare tempo too soon, and then he can shadow your king forever. } 2... Ke5 3. Kf3 3... Kf5 4. Ke3 4... Ke5 { The result has changed: he mirrors every king move, and without spending the pawn tempo you can never force him aside. [%cal Rf5e5] }) 2... Kf6 { His king steps aside to f6 rather than meet yours. Walk straight past him now: king to d5. } 3. Kd5 { Your king slips past his and takes d5. The pawn can wait on e3: the king travels first. [%cal Ge4d5] } (3. Kd4 { Heading to d4 instead of d5 makes no real difference: your king still slips through, with the pawn tempo unspent. } 3... Ke6 { Also winning. Your king can go here as well; the reserve pawn move is still waiting. [%cal Bf6e6] }) (3. Kf4 { Your king could aim for f4 instead of d5. Either square keeps the win, and the reserve pawn move still waits. } 3... Kg7 { Also winning. This square also wins, and you still hold the spare pawn move. [%cal Bf6g7] }) (3. Kd3 { Stepping to d3 retreats instead of advancing, and it hands the ground your king just won straight back to him. } 3... Ke5 4. Kd2 4... Kf6 5. Ke2 5... Ke6 { The result has changed: retreating gives back the ground your king had gained. [%cal Rf6e6] }) (3. Kf3 { Your king wanders to f3 instead of pressing on, and that lets his king slip back in front of the pawn. } 3... Ke7 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 5. Kf4 5... Kf6 { The result has changed: wandering like this lets his king get back in front of your pawn and block it. [%cal Re6f6] }) 3... Ke7 { He hurries back to block the road. Stand right in front of him: king to e5. [%cal Yd5e5] } 4. Ke5 { The kings look straight at each other with one square between them, and it is his turn to move. He has to step aside. [%csl Ge5,Re7] } (4. e4 { The pawn charges to e4 alone, but your king was supposed to lead the way here, not follow behind it. } 4... Kd7 5. e5 5... Ke7 6. e6 { His king parks in front of the pawn on e7 and your king can never shift it. The win is gone. [%csl Re7] }) (4. Kc5 { Your king strays to c5 instead of standing its ground, and that hands the whole road back to him. } 4... Ke6 5. e4 5... Ke5 6. Kb4 { His king stands square in front of your pawn on e5 and holds it there for ever. Only a draw. [%csl Re5] }) (4. Kd4 { Backwards to d4 undoes everything: the square your king just won goes straight back to his king. } 4... Kd6 5. e4 5... Ke6 6. e5 { He holds the square in front of your pawn and your king never gets ahead of it again. Drawn. [%csl Re6] }) (4. Ke4 { Your king steps back to e4, right behind its own pawn, and that stops the pawn dead in its tracks. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd3 5... Kd5 6. e4+ { Your pawn checks, he steps away and strolls back, and your king has gained nothing at all. Drawn. [%csl Rd5] }) 4... Kd7 { He gives way to d7, so go around him the other way this time: king to f6, and the pawn can finally walk. [%cal Ye5f6] } 5. Kf6 { Around the other side, and your king is past his once more. Now the pawn can walk, with your king in front of it the whole way. [%cal Ge5f6][%tail] } (5. e4 { The pawn charges to e4 by itself again, exactly what he is hoping for while your king still is not in front of it. } 5... Ke7 6. Kd4 6... Kd6 7. e5+ { Your pawn checks from in front of your own king, and his king settles down in its path. Drawn. [%csl Re5] }) (5. Kd5 { Also winning, and the kings face off once more. But stepping around to f6 is quicker. } 5... Ke7 6. Ke5 6... Kd7 7. Kf6 { You reach f6 in the end anyway, just two moves later than the direct road would have taken. [%cal Ge5f6] }) (5. Kf5 { This also wins: your king still goes round him, just by the longer road through f5 instead of straight to f6. } 5... Ke7 { He faces you again on e7, and you must spend one more move crossing over to f6 to get past him. [%cal Bf5f6] }) (5. Kf4 { Winning as well, but your king drops back a row to f4 for no reason at all: f6 was one move away. } 5... Ke6 6. Ke4 6... Kd6 7. Kf5 { Same plan, same squares in the end, just one extra lap of the board wasted getting there. [%cal Bf4f5] }) 5... Kc6 6. e4 6... Kd7 7. e5 7... Ke8 8. Ke6 { King first: pushing e6 would let his king block it. } 8... Kd8 9. Kf7 9... Kc7 10. e6 10... Kb6 11. e7 11... Kc5 12. e8=Q { A new queen on e8. One spare pawn move at the start, and your king led the pawn home. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Walk around the pawn wall"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/6p1/4K1P1/5P2/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
