{ The pawn push looks tempting, but is there a better route? See if your king can prepare the ground first. } 1. Kd7 { Your king takes a detour through d7 instead of pushing pawns. From there it reaches e7 without ever touching the g6 pawn. [%cal Ge6d7] } (1. Kd6 { Your king can go by way of d6 instead of d7. Either door around the pawns leads to the same win. } 1... Ke8 { Also winning. Your king still finds a way around the pawn wall. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (1. Kd5 { Another good square, d5: your king still finds a door round the wall from over there. } 1... Ke7 { This wins too. Your king reaches the far side of the wall by another path. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. Ke5 { Your king can swing through e5 instead of d7. Any of these routes still gets round the wall. } 1... Ke7 { Still winning: your king goes around the wall from here as well. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. f6 { The pawn advances to f6 before your king has reached any of the useful squares it needs first. } 1... Kg8 2. f7+ 2... Kf8 3. Ke5 3... Ke7 { The result has changed: his king slips into e7 first and blocks the way your king needed around the wall. [%cal Rf8e7] }) 1... Kg8 { His king steps back to g8. Where can yours go next to claim the important square around the pawns? } 2. Ke7 { Your king reaches e7. Now the f pawn can help open a route to promotion. [%cal Gd7e7] } (2. Ke8 { The e8 square works just as well as e7 for your king: either one slips past the pawns in time. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning. Your king slips around the pawns just as well this way. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Kd6 { Going to d6 costs you nothing here: your king still finds its way around the wall. } 2... Kf8 { This route wins too, and your king still gets around the wall. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kd8 { Your king has another good try in d8: it still finds a door past the pawns from there. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning. Your king finds another door around the pawn wall. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Ke6 { Your king can go to e6 instead of e7. It still wins, just by a slightly slower road. } 2... Kf8 { Still good: your king still circles round the wall in time to reach the squares it needs. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc6 { The square c6 works just as well for your king as c7 or e7: any door around the wall wins. } 2... Kh8 { This also wins. Your king reaches the far side of the wall regardless. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Kc7 { Heading to c7 is fine too: your king slips around from the far side just as well. } 2... Kf8 { Also winning. Another path around the wall works for your king. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc8 { Your king could step to c8 instead of e7: the corner falls to your king from either side. } 2... Kh8 { This wins as well. Your king still turns the corner past the pawns. [%cal Bg8h8] }) 2... Kh8 { His king is jammed into the corner on h8, with nowhere else to go. Now break that pawn wall open. } 3. f6 { Your pawn jabs into f6, right where his g pawn can take it. That is the idea: the wall comes apart. [%cal Gf5f6] } (3. Kf7 { Careful! Your king takes the last square from his king: the one on h8 cannot move and the g pawn is blocked. Stalemate, and the win is gone. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Kf8 { Squeezing in to f8 leaves his king on h8 with nowhere at all to go, and the blocked g pawn cannot move either. Stalemate. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Ke6 { Your king drops back to e6 instead of breaking the wall now, but the win just waits a move longer. } 3... Kg8 { Also winning, but the wall is untouched and your king has to come back. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd6 { The d6 square keeps everything you have: the same win, just reached a move later than f7. } 3... Kg8 { His king returns to g8 and the pawn wall still stands, waiting for the break. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd7 { A quiet step to d7. The win survives, though nothing has been asked of him. } 3... Kg8 { He walks back out to g8, and the f pawn break is still waiting to be played. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd8 { Your king strolls to d8 instead. Still winning, but the wall of pawns has not been touched yet. } 3... Kg8 { Out he comes to g8 again, and you have spent a move for nothing. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Ke8 { The e8 square wins too, but it never asks his king a single question about the pawns. } 3... Kg8 { He steps back to g8, and the pawns on f5 and g6 are exactly where they were. [%cal Bh8g8] }) 3... gxf6 { His g pawn takes on f6. There is a free pawn there for your king, and one very tempting push. Choose carefully. } 4. Kxf6 { Your king scoops up f6. His king on h8 is alone now, and your g pawn has an escort. [%cal Ge7f6] } (4. g7+ { A check, and the pawn is one square from a queen. Look again before you play it. } 4... Kxg7 { His king just takes on g7. Your last pawn is gone, his f pawn is still marching, and now you are the one losing. [%cal Rh8g7] }) (4. Kf7 { Also winning, and even quicker than taking: your king guards g8. [%cal Gf7g8] } 4... f5 { Still winning. His pawn runs, but there is nothing for it to catch. [%cal Bf6f5] }) (4. Kf8 { The f8 square wins as well for your king, just one move slower than simply collecting the pawn. } 4... f5 { This wins too. His pawn is running with nobody at all to help it. [%cal Bf6f5] }) 4... Kg8 { Out he comes to g8 to stand in the pawn's way. Pawn or king: which of yours goes now? } 5. g7 { The pawn steps up to g7, and your king on f6 stands guard over it. His king cannot touch it there. [%cal Gg6g7] } (5. Ke6 { Your king wanders off to e6 and stops watching the pawn on g6, and that pawn is the one doing the winning. } 5... Kg7 { His king lands on g7, where it attacks the pawn on g6 and wins it. Only a draw now. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (5. Ke7 { The e7 square leaves the pawn on g6 with nobody looking after it, and his king only needs one move to grab it. } 5... Kg7 { Straight to g7 he goes, and the pawn on g6 is his. The win is thrown away. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (5. Kf5 { Dropping back to f5 takes your king off guard duty one move too soon, and the pawn on g6 pays for it. } 5... Kg7 { His king reaches g7 and the pawn on g6 falls. That leaves a draw. [%cal Rg8g7] }) 5... Kh7 { His king has only h7. There is a queen one square away, but look where his king is standing. } 6. Kf7 { Your king takes f7 and covers g8. Now the pawn can turn into a queen with nobody able to touch her. [%tail] [%cal Gf6f7,Gf7g8] } (6. g8=Q+ { A queen appears on g8, with check! But look who is standing right beside her already. } 6... Kxg8 { His king on h7 was standing right beside g8 the whole time. He takes her, and bare kings mean a draw. [%csl Rg8] }) (6. Ke6 { Your king steps away from g7 and the pawn on g6 is suddenly on its own, with nobody to stop it being taken. } 6... Kxg7 { His king helps itself to the pawn on g7. Nothing is left on the board, so it is a draw. [%csl Rg7] }) (6. Kf5 { Back to f5, and there is nothing at all guarding the pawn on g7 now: his king simply walks up and takes it. } 6... Kxg7 { He collects the pawn on g7 and the board is bare. The win has evaporated. [%csl Rg7] }) 6... Kh6 7. g8=Q { Queen on g8! The wall on f5 and g6 is long gone, and his king on h6 watched your king walk right around it. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


