{ You could rush toward the b pawn. Can you stop his king from taking your h pawn first? } 4. Kf4 { You keep your king right in front of his. Racing past would let him attack your h pawn instead. [%tail] [%cal Ge4f4] } (4. Kd4 { Your king steps away toward d4, but that abandons the h pawn, and his king slips around to grab it. } 4... Kf5 5. Kc5 5... Kg4 6. Kxb5 6... Kxh4 { The result has changed: this lets his king slip past toward your h pawn. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Kd5 { You race for the b pawn by way of d5, but his king races just as fast the other way, toward your h pawn. } 4... Kf5 5. Kc6 5... Kg4 6. Kxb5 6... Kxh4 { Giving up the block here lets his king slip through to your h pawn, and the result has changed. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Kd3 { Your king retreats to d3 and gives up the square that was blocking his king from advancing. } 4... Kf5 5. Kd4 5... Kg4 6. Kc5 6... Kxh4 { The result changes here: with your king off the block, his simply strolls around it and grabs the h pawn. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Ke3 { Stepping to e3 lets his king take the block instead: now it is his king pushing yours back. } 4... Ke5 5. Kf3 5... Kf5 6. Ke3 6... Ke5 { You lose the block this way: his king simply mirrors yours back and forth, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf5e5] }) 4... Ke6 5. Kg5 5... Ke5 6. Kxh5 { Your king takes on h5. You are a pawn up with a free h pawn, and his king on e5 cannot watch that and his own b pawn at the same time. [%cal Gg5h5] } 1-0


