{ The pawn is close to promotion, but which must lead the way now: your king, or the pawn itself? } 8. Ke6 { Your king takes the key square. Pushing the pawn first would give away the win. [%cal Gf6e6] } (8. e6 { The pawn gets ahead of your king here, and that lets his king plant itself right in front and hold it. } 8... Kf8 9. Kg6 9... Ke8 10. Kf5 10... Ke7 { His king blocks the pawn cold on e7. You needed to lead with your own king instead. [%cal Re8e7] }) (8. Kf5 { The square f5 looks close enough, but it drops off the key diagonal your king needs, and the result changes. } 8... Kf7 9. Kg4 9... Kg6 10. Kf4 10... Kf7 { The result has changed: his king reaches the key square first this way. [%cal Rg6f7] }) (8. Kg6 { This king move to g6 overshoots the key square by a full column, and the result changes. } 8... Ke7 9. Kg5 9... Ke6 10. Kf4 10... Kd7 { This move hands him the promotion route, and the result has changed. [%cal Re6d7] }) (8. Kg5 { Try g5 instead of e6, and it misses the key square by one row: the result flips against you. } 8... Kf7 9. Kf4 9... Kg6 10. Kg4 10... Kg7 { The result changes here, because your king arrives one move too late. [%cal Rg6g7] }) 8... Kf8 { His king yields a square by stepping back. Keep the promotion route firmly under your control now. } 9. Kd7 { Your king grabs d7 and owns the promotion square. Now watch the pawn stroll up behind it. [%tail] [%cal Ge6d7] } (9. Kf6 { Your king can travel through f6 instead of d7: either route still claims the key square first. } 9... Ke8 { Also winning: your king still controls the promotion route from f6 just as well as from d7. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (9. Kd6 { Another good square, d6: your king still beats his there to the promotion route. } 9... Kf7 { This wins too: your king still reaches the key square first, whichever door it takes there. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (9. Kd5 { Your king to d5 looks reasonable, but it drops off the promotion route, and the result changes. } 9... Ke7 10. Ke4 10... Kf7 11. Ke3 11... Ke6 { You lose control of the route this way, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (9. Kf5 { Going to f5 instead of d7 lets his king slip back onto the key square, costing you the result. } 9... Kf7 10. Kg4 10... Kg6 11. Kf4 11... Kf7 { The result has changed: the promotion square needed your king there first. [%cal Rg6f7] }) 9... Kf7 10. e6+ 10... Kf6 11. e7 11... Ke5 12. e8=Q+ { A new queen on e8, with check. Your king went first the whole way, and his king on e5 never got in front of the pawn. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: One square, not two"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1_b_-_-_1_8"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/qtmFvp4T"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1 b - - 1 8"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "King and tempo"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 23, critical position with the white king on g1 so that the single step is the winner"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your g pawn can open with one step or two, and only one of them wins. Count the race before you push. } 8... g6 { One step only. The second one stays in your pocket until the moment it is worth something. [%cal Gg7g6] } (8... g5 { Two squares at once looks like the quicker road. Watch what his king does with the corner. } 9. Kh1 9... g4 10. Kg1 10... g3 11. hxg3 11... hxg3 12. Kh1 12... g2+ 13. Kg1 13... Kg3 { Stalemate! Your pawn on g2 shuts f1 and h1, your king on g3 covers the rest, and he has no move at all. The rush threw the win away. [%csl Rg1] }) (8... Kg4 { Your king goes hunting on g4 while both pawns just sit there, and that gives his king time to get out. } 9. Kg2 9... h3+ 10. Kf1 10... g6 11. Ke1 11... g5 { His king walked out to e1 while you were busy, and from there it holds everything. Drawn. [%csl Re1] }) 9. Kh1 { His king stays pinned in the corner, right beside the h2 pawn that it has to sit and guard forever. } 9... g5 { Now the step you saved. His king must move, and the corner leaves it almost nowhere. [%cal Gg6g5] } (9... Kg4 { Your king steps out to g4 and lets his king slip out of the corner right along with it. } 10. Kg2 10... h3+ 11. Kf1 11... g5 12. Ke1 { He is out of the corner and home on e1 in time to stop both of your pawns cold. Drawn. [%csl Re1] }) 10. Kg1 { His king is squeezed to g1: the corner and h1 are both covered, so that is the only square left. } 10... g4 { The pawn keeps coming to g4, and his king has no choice but to go straight back to the corner. [%cal Gg5g4] } (10... Kg4 { The only other move he has here, and it frees his king from the corner right when you need it trapped. } 11. Kg2 11... h3+ 12. Kf1 12... Kf3 13. Kg1 { His king is out of the corner for good, and your pawns have simply stopped moving. Drawn. [%csl Rg1] }) 11. Kh1 { Back to the corner he goes. He is shuttling helplessly between two squares while your pawn marches on. } 11... g3 { Now he has to take: the pawn on h2 cannot move and his king cannot leave it. [%cal Gg4g3] } 12. hxg3 { He takes on g3, because there was nothing else left to do. Now, which of your pawns takes back? } 12... hxg3 { The h pawn takes, and your king on h3 stands right beside the new pawn on g3. [%cal Gh4g3] } (12... Kxg3 { The king takes on g3 instead of the pawn, and your last runner becomes a lone rook pawn. } 13. Kg1 13... h3 14. Kh1 14... h2 { Stalemate again, and this time his king never even had to work: a rook pawn with his king in front of it is the oldest draw there is. [%csl Rh1] }) 13. Kg1 { His king is squeezed back to g1 again: every other square is covered, so it has no choice at all. } 13... g2 { The pawn steps to g2 and takes f1 and h1 away from him. [%cal Gg3g2] } (13... Kg4 { Your king wanders off to g4 and leaves the pawn to walk home alone, with nobody there to guard it. } 14. Kg2 14... Kf4 15. Kg1 15... g2 16. Kxg2 { His king simply strolls over and collects the pawn on g2. Two bare kings left, and the win is gone. [%csl Rg2] }) 14. Kf2 { He is pushed out to f2, the only square left for him. Now, which goes first: the pawn, or the king? } 14... Kh2 { Your king guards the pawn on g2, and now nothing can touch it. Watch it home. [%tail][%cal Gh3h2] } (14... g1=Q+ { A new queen on g1, with check! But look carefully at the square she has just landed on. } 15. Kxg1 { His king takes her on g1, one move after you were completely winning. [%csl Rg1] }) 15. Ke1 15... g1=Q+ { A new queen on g1, with check. That single step at the start kept the second one in reserve, and the spare move won the race. [%csl Gg1] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca endings: Bring the king up to the majority"]
