{ You are a pawn down. Hold the draw by finding a distant way to keep the kings opposed. } 1. Kh1 { The corner gives you distant opposition. Your pawn no longer blocks your route. [%cal Gg2h1] } (1. Kh3 { Your king heading to h3 instead of the corner throws the result away: it steps off the column that matters. } 1... Ke1 2. Kg3 2... Kf1 3. Kg4 3... Ke2 { The result has changed: leaving the h column costs you the distant opposition. [%cal Rf1e2] }) (1. Kf1 { Close opposition looks right at first, but your own pawn will block the very next step you need. } 1... Kd2 2. Kg1 2... Kd3 3. Kf1 3... Ke3 { Your own pawn blocks the lateral route here, so the close opposition simply does not hold. [%cal Rd3e3] }) (1. Kf2 { The square f2 seems fine, but it drops off the h column, losing the distant opposition. } 1... Kd2 2. Kf1 2... Ke3 3. Kg2 3... Ke2 { This steps off the h column, and the result has changed. The opposition is his now. [%cal Re3e2] }) (1. Kg3 { This king move to g3 is a step too far off the corner already, and the result changes. } 1... Ke1 2. Kh3 2... Kf2 3. Kg4 3... Kg2 { The result changes here, because your king abandons the h column he needed. [%cal Rf2g2] }) 1... Kd2 { His king moves up to challenge yours. Can you keep the exact same spacing between the two kings? } 2. Kh2 { Your king follows him up the h column, leaving the right amount of room beside your pawn. [%cal Gh1h2] } (2. Kg1 { Try g1 instead of h2, and your king steps off the h column that keeps the distance right. } 2... Kd3 3. Kf1 3... Ke3 4. Kg2 4... Ke2 { Losing the h column loses the distant opposition, and the result has changed. [%cal Re3e2] }) (2. Kg2 { Your king to g2 looks reasonable, but it leaves the h column, and the result changes. } 2... Ke2 3. Kg3 3... Kd1 4. Kh3 4... Ke1 { The result has changed: his king takes the opposition once you leave the h column. [%cal Rd1e1] }) (2. f4 { Try f4 with the pawn instead of the king, and his king slips into the h column you needed. } 2... exf4 3. Kh2 3... Ke2 4. Kh3 4... Kf3 { This gives up the h column your king was holding, and with it the result changes. [%cal Re2f3] }) 2... Kd3 { He advances again, still hunting for a way through. Keep the lateral opposition facing him. } 3. Kh3 { Your king lands on h3 and still faces his king from a distance, with the whole h column to itself. [%cal Gh2h3] } (3. Kg3 { Going to g3 instead of h3 costs you the h column, and with it the distant opposition. } 3... Ke3 4. Kg2 4... Ke2 5. Kg3 5... Kf1 { The result has changed. The h column was the only route to distant opposition. [%cal Re2f1] }) (3. Kg1 { The g1 square is tempting, but it steps off the h column, and the result changes there. } 3... Kc3 4. Kg2 4... Kc2 5. Kg3 5... Kd1 { Off the h column, your king loses the opposition, and the result has changed. [%cal Rc2d1] }) (3. Kh1 { Your king's route through h1 backtracks a row it cannot spare, and the result changes. } 3... Kc3 4. Kg2 4... Kc2 5. Kg3 5... Kd1 { The result changes the moment your king steps away from the h column it needed to hold. [%cal Rc2d1] }) (3. Kg2 { This time g2 is the wrong square for your king: it gives up the h column, and the result changes. } 3... Ke2 4. Kg3 4... Kd1 5. Kf2 5... Kd2 { This square costs you the h column your king was guarding, and the result has changed. [%cal Rd1d2] }) 3... e4 { He throws the e pawn at your f pawn, hoping to open lines. Only one answer holds the position. } 4. fxe4 { Your pawn takes on e4. With the pawns coming off there is nothing left on the board for him to win with. [%tail] [%cal Gf3e4] } (4. f4 { You push past him instead of capturing, and now it is his pawns doing the running, not yours. } 4... gxf4 5. Kg2 5... e3 6. Kf1 6... f3 { Two pawns on e3 and f3 with his king on d3 right there with them. You cannot hold both. [%cal Rf3f2] }) (4. Kg2 { Stepping back to g2 instead of capturing leaves the pawn on e4 alive and kicking, free to run. } 4... e3 5. Kf1 5... e2+ 6. Kf2 6... Kd2 { The pawn on e2 has his king beside it on d2, and nothing of yours can touch it. [%cal Re2e1] }) (4. Kg4 { Going after the g pawn instead costs you the one move you simply did not have to spare here. } 4... e3 5. Kg3 5... e2 6. Kf2 6... Kd2 { His king on d2 walks the e pawn in, and your king arrives a move late. That is a loss. [%cal Re2e1] }) 4... Kxe4 5. Kg4 5... Kd3 6. Kxg5 { Your king captures the last pawn on g5. Two bare kings and an empty board: the game is a draw. [%csl Gg5] } 1-0


