{ He is two pawns up and his f pawn is running: you are playing for the draw. Keep the rook active and play it to d6, so it can reach d8 first. [%cal Yd3d6] } 1... Rd6 { Your rook takes the d column. It can reach the back row before the pawn queens. [%cal Gd3d6] } (1... Rd5 { Your rook can go to d5 instead of d6: either square still reaches d8 in time to stop the pawn. } 2. Rb7 { Also drawing: your rook still reaches d8 in time to stop the pawn, just by a different road. [%cal Bh7b7] }) (1... Kd6 { Your king can take the d6 path instead of the rook: it still gets to the back row in time. } 2. Rb7 { This holds the draw too: your rook still gets back to d8 in time, whichever path it takes. [%cal Bh7b7] }) (1... Kc6 { This king move to c6 is a step too far from the back row when the rook needed help, and the result changes. } 2. Re7 2... Rd5 3. Re4 3... b3 4. h4 { The result has changed: your rook never reaches d8 to guard the promotion square. [%cal Rh2h4] }) (1... Rd4 { Your rook can also try d4 instead of d6, but from there it cannot reach d8 in time, and the result changes. } 2. Rb7 2... Rg4+ 3. Kf3 3... Rg6 4. f7 { This move leaves d8 completely open for the pawn, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf6f7] }) (1... Rd2 { Your rook moves away from the back-row defense entirely, and now nothing stops the pawn queening. } 2. Rb7 2... Rd8 3. f7 3... Kd6 4. h4 { The result changes here, because the rook cannot get back to stop promotion. [%cal Rh2h4] }) 2. f7 { His pawn advances to f7. Move your rook to d8 now, right in front of where it wants to queen. [%cal Yd6d8] } 2... Rd8 { Your rook reaches d8. It guards f8 while your own b pawn keeps him busy, and the position is a draw. [%cal Gd6d8,Gd8f8] } (2... Rf6 { The square f6 works just as well for your rook as d6: either one still reaches d8 in time. } 3. Rg7 { Still drawing: the rook reaches d8 from g7 just as surely as from d6. [%cal Bh7g7] }) (2... Rg6+ { The g6 square looks natural for the rook, but it never gets back in time, and the result changes. } 3. Kf1 3... Rf6 4. Ke2 4... Kd6 5. Rh6 { Without the rook on d8, his pawn queens safely, and the result has changed. [%cal Rh7h6] }) (2... Kd5 { Try d5 with the king instead of sending the rook to d8, and the back-row defense arrives too late. } 3. f8=Q 3... Rg6+ 4. Kh1 4... Rd6 5. Rh5+ { The result has changed: the back-row defense simply arrives on d8 too late to matter. [%cal Rh7h5] }) (2... Kc6 { Your king to c6 looks reasonable, but the rook still needed to reach d8, and the result changes. } 3. f8=Q 3... Rg6+ 4. Kf3 4... Rd6 5. Qa8+ { This way your rook never makes it to d8 at all, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf8a8] }) 0-1


