{ You are two pawns down and his rook checks: hold the draw. Which king move keeps your counterplay alive? } 2... Kd4 { Your king stays close to the b pawn. Stepping back would lose that counterplay. [%cal Gc5d4] } (2... Kd5 { Heading to d5 is fine too: your king still stays close enough to help the b pawn push through. } 3. f7 { Also drawing: your king still keeps in close touch with the b pawn from over there. [%cal Bf6f7] }) (2... Kb6 { Your king retreats to b6, too far away now from the passed b pawn it needed to escort. } 3. f7 3... Rg6+ 4. Kf1 4... Rf6 5. Rc4 { Your king has lost contact with the b pawn. The active king was essential. [%cal Rc7c4] }) (2... Kb5 { Going to b5 instead of d4 drifts your king too far from the b pawn, costing you the result. } 3. Rb7+ 3... Kc5 4. f7 4... Rg6+ 5. Kf3 { The result has changed: your king drifts too far from the b pawn, and he catches it. [%cal Rg2f3] }) (2... Rc6 { Moving the rook to c6 instead of keeping your king central lets him trade it off, and the result changes. } 3. Rxc6+ 3... Kxc6 4. f7 4... Kb5 5. Kg3 { This gives up contact with the b pawn for good, and the result has changed. [%cal Rg2g3] }) 3. f7 { His pawn advances to f7. Find a check first that gains you a useful move before you stop it. } 3... Rg6+ { The check gains time and keeps your king active. The position is a draw. [%cal Gd6g6] } (3... Rf6 { This rook route through f6 instead of g6 keeps the result exactly the same: your king stays in touch. } 4. Rb7 { This holds the draw too. Your king stays close enough to help the passer. [%cal Bc7b7] }) (3... Rd8 { This rook route through d8 throws the result away: it gives up the check that gains a move. } 4. Rd7+ 4... Rxd7 5. f8=Q { Your rook remains on the board, but he has a new queen. The drawing defense is lost. [%cal Rf7f8] }) (3... Ke5 { The e5 square is tempting for your king, but it drifts away from the b pawn, and the result changes. } 4. f8=Q 4... Re6 5. Qg7+ 5... Kd5 6. Qd7+ { The result changes here, because your king can no longer help the passer. [%cal Rg7d7] }) (3... Rd5 { The square d5 seems fine for the rook, but it loses touch with the b pawn, and the result changes here. } 4. f8=Q 4... Ke4 5. Qf6 5... Re5 6. Rd7 { You lose touch with the b pawn this way, and with it the result has changed. [%cal Rc7d7] }) 0-1


